t required when NAGS activity and/or NAG levels are sufficient to activate the first step of the urea cycle.<b>NEW & NOTEWORTHY</b> Hepatic ureagenesis is essential in amino acid metabolism and is importantly regulated by glucagon, but the exact mechanism is unclear. With the aim to identify the steps whereby glucagon both acutely and chronically regulates ureagenesis, we here show, contrary to our hypothesis, that glucagon receptor-mediated activation of ureagenesis is not required when <i>N</i>-acetylglutamate synthase activity and/or <i>N</i>-acetylglutamate levels are sufficient to activate the first step of the urea cycle in vivo.
